1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:10,000
Throughout history, mankind's most spectacular achievements and his worst disasters have come from the same hand.

2
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:16,000
Engineers make modern life possible, but when they fail, thousands can die.

3
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:20,000
Now, Engineering Disasters on Modern Marvels.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
Major disasters occur because there's a whole string of events that happen in a certain order,

5
00:00:44,380 --> 00:00:48,460
any one of which, had they not happened, the disaster wouldn't have happened.

6
00:00:48,580 --> 00:00:51,060
We'd like it to stir up your cryo tank.

7
00:00:51,420 --> 00:00:54,060
You figure that nothing is perfect. Everything's going to fail.

8
00:00:54,061 --> 00:01:00,380
Two or three failures that interact in a way that nobody anticipated bring the system down.

9
00:01:01,260 --> 00:01:08,480
Some of our most soaring triumphs as a race.

10
00:01:08,960 --> 00:01:14,140
The pyramids, the Parthenon, the shining towers of a modern city.

11
00:01:15,320 --> 00:01:19,300
Public building can be the most impressive and memorable of engineering feats,

12
00:01:19,301 --> 00:01:22,240
and the most spectacular when it fails.

13
00:01:24,060 --> 00:01:29,660
Engineering disasters are nothing new.

14
00:01:30,240 --> 00:01:33,440
Over 4,000 years ago, the oldest written law book,

15
00:01:33,540 --> 00:01:36,020
The Code of Hammurabi from ancient Babylon,

16
00:01:36,520 --> 00:01:40,340
provided penalties that represented the world's first building codes.

17
00:01:43,540 --> 00:01:47,620
If a builder build a house for someone and does not construct it properly,

18
00:01:48,100 --> 00:01:50,980
and the house which he built fall in and kill its owner,

19
00:01:51,480 --> 00:01:53,440
then that builder shall be put to death.

20
00:01:54,060 --> 00:01:58,380
If it kill the son of the owner, the son of that builder shall be put to death.

21
00:01:59,040 --> 00:02:04,440
If it kill a slave of the owner, then he shall pay slave for slave to the owner of the house.

22
00:02:05,040 --> 00:02:09,780
If it ruin goods, he shall make compensation for all that has been ruined.

23
00:02:12,120 --> 00:02:16,060
In 305 B.C., after a military victory,

24
00:02:16,061 --> 00:02:20,820
the citizens of ancient Rhodes commissioned the largest statue ever built

25
00:02:20,821 --> 00:02:24,240
as tribute to their protector, the god Apollo.

26
00:02:25,260 --> 00:02:29,280
Built of solid bronze by the sculptor Chares of Lindos,

27
00:02:29,281 --> 00:02:34,040
the massive monument and its base rose 150 feet above the harbor,

28
00:02:34,640 --> 00:02:37,040
about the same height as the modern Statue of Liberty.

29
00:02:37,600 --> 00:02:40,020
It took eight years to build, and when it was finished,

30
00:02:40,120 --> 00:02:43,880
the Colossus of Rhodes was one of the seven wonders of the world.

31
00:02:45,080 --> 00:02:49,040
But Chares and his engineers were only guessing at the stresses involved.

32
00:02:49,720 --> 00:02:52,420
In an earthquake in the 2nd century B.C.,

33
00:02:52,421 --> 00:02:56,340
the mighty statue cracked at the knees and fell to earth.

34
00:02:57,860 --> 00:03:01,120
Throughout history, the curious want to know what happens

35
00:03:01,121 --> 00:03:04,480
when you try to build a little farther, a little lighter,

36
00:03:04,740 --> 00:03:06,480
a little higher than anyone else.

37
00:03:06,900 --> 00:03:09,520
Oftentimes, the answer is disaster.

38
00:03:11,520 --> 00:03:14,660
In the Middle Ages, the Gothic cathedrals of France

39
00:03:14,661 --> 00:03:17,640
sent thin spidery spires straight up into the heavens.

40
00:03:19,040 --> 00:03:26,040
The citizens of Beauvais, France wanted their cathedral to be taller and more beautiful than any that had come before.

41
00:03:29,040 --> 00:03:38,040
Once again, the medieval engineers were pushing the envelope, building higher than anyone else, against forces they didn't fully understand.

42
00:03:39,040 --> 00:03:43,040
At 157 feet, it was the highest roof in Europe at the time.

43
00:03:43,041 --> 00:03:52,140
But with only a spidery internal network of iron tie rods for support,

44
00:03:52,420 --> 00:03:54,100
the result was disaster.

45
00:03:54,960 --> 00:03:58,000
In 1284, the roof collapsed into the choir.

46
00:04:01,280 --> 00:04:04,140
After the collapse, the cathedral was rebuilt

47
00:04:04,141 --> 00:04:08,240
with additional heavy stone piers holding the roof up from the foundation.

48
00:04:08,241 --> 00:04:15,020
Though there was never enough money to complete it

49
00:04:15,021 --> 00:04:16,880
according to the grandiose plans

50
00:04:16,881 --> 00:04:21,020
the Beauvais Cathedral has been called the Parthenon of France

51
00:04:21,021 --> 00:04:26,020
Another building most famous for its engineering failure

52
00:04:26,021 --> 00:04:30,080
is a bell tower constructed in Italy beginning in 1174

53
00:04:30,081 --> 00:04:34,420
Its foundation was so flawed it became world famous

54
00:04:34,421 --> 00:04:36,560
as the Leaning Tower of Pisa

55
00:04:36,561 --> 00:04:39,560
Today the tower is closed.

56
00:04:39,561 --> 00:04:42,560
Massive lead counterweights are slowing its collapse

57
00:04:42,561 --> 00:04:46,560
and local engineers have devised a system of cables to hold it up

58
00:04:46,561 --> 00:04:49,560
while they try to repair its foundation.

59
00:04:54,560 --> 00:04:59,560
In the next 800 years, engineering passed from an art into a science,

60
00:04:59,561 --> 00:05:03,560
and the achievements and failures of engineers became truly breathtaking.

61
00:05:06,560 --> 00:05:11,700
When the Tacoma Narrows Bridge in Washington State opened in 1940,

62
00:05:11,701 --> 00:05:16,020
it was thought to be lovely and elegant in the new lightweight style,

63
00:05:16,380 --> 00:05:18,600
but nothing very groundbreaking.

64
00:05:19,660 --> 00:05:22,360
It was a wonderful bridge to just look at. It was beautiful.

65
00:05:23,920 --> 00:05:29,140
The spectacular Golden Gate Bridge in San Francisco had opened three years before,

66
00:05:29,700 --> 00:05:32,060
with a span almost 2,000 feet longer.

67
00:05:32,061 --> 00:05:39,560
On the other coast, New York's George Washington Bridge, built in 1933, was 700 feet longer.

68
00:05:40,300 --> 00:05:43,880
But the Tacoma Narrows Bridge would make a lasting name for itself.

69
00:05:44,420 --> 00:05:48,420
Within days of its opening, it began to sway like a hammock in the breeze.

70
00:05:49,180 --> 00:05:51,760
Pretty soon it was the most famous bridge in America.

71
00:05:53,040 --> 00:06:01,480
We learned about the bridge that when the wind was up, it would become galloping gertie,

72
00:06:01,481 --> 00:06:02,640
move up and down.

73
00:06:03,140 --> 00:06:04,880
And when the wind was really bad,

74
00:06:04,940 --> 00:06:06,580
we used to ride our bikes out there,

75
00:06:06,980 --> 00:06:09,560
park on a hill beside the bridge

76
00:06:09,561 --> 00:06:12,080
and watch it go up and down just for a lark.

77
00:06:12,940 --> 00:06:15,900
They let people go when the dropping was so sudden

78
00:06:15,901 --> 00:06:18,300
that it felt like an old-fashioned elevator.

79
00:06:19,240 --> 00:06:19,940
Boom.

80
00:06:20,260 --> 00:06:20,960
Like that.

81
00:06:21,860 --> 00:06:25,460
Galloping Gertie remained open to traffic for four months.

82
00:06:25,960 --> 00:06:28,600
But in November, everybody was ordered off

83
00:06:28,601 --> 00:06:32,100
as a 40-mile-an-hour gale whipped across her deck,

84
00:06:32,420 --> 00:06:34,540
lifting the roadway like a sail.

85
00:06:35,880 --> 00:06:39,760
We went out to watch it whip, and it was really whipping,

86
00:06:39,980 --> 00:06:41,280
and there was one car on it.

87
00:06:41,540 --> 00:06:43,480
But then it got late, and so we left.

88
00:06:44,180 --> 00:06:46,700
By afternoon, the stresses were too much.

89
00:06:47,340 --> 00:06:50,420
Incredibly, the 11,000-ton center span

90
00:06:50,421 --> 00:06:52,760
ripped away and crashed into the sea.

91
00:06:58,600 --> 00:07:05,280
Aerodynamic problems had never been considered important before.

92
00:07:05,281 --> 00:07:11,260
Every bridge ever designed, even the smallest arch over a stream,

93
00:07:12,340 --> 00:07:16,420
has some aerodynamic lift associated with that span, some aerodynamic characteristics.

94
00:07:16,980 --> 00:07:21,740
Well, if the bridge is stone and it's 20 feet long, how important are the aerodynamic forces?

95
00:07:23,440 --> 00:07:24,300
They're not.

96
00:07:24,301 --> 00:07:29,120
But the Tacoma Narrows Bridge was no stone arch over a stream.

97
00:07:30,040 --> 00:07:33,120
The center span was 2,800 feet long.

98
00:07:33,920 --> 00:07:39,180
Ironically, the major factor in the spectacular collapse was that bridge designers were getting better.

99
00:07:40,580 --> 00:07:42,620
Bridges were no longer, thinner, and lighter.

100
00:07:43,300 --> 00:07:51,240
As that trend continued, inevitably, the secondary effect of aerodynamics on a bridge one day became a primary effect.

101
00:07:51,241 --> 00:07:57,240
The aerodynamics are always there, but as you get longer and longer and longer, there's just more profile,

102
00:07:58,260 --> 00:08:02,700
and your bridge is getting less proportionately stiff to length as you're getting longer and longer spans,

103
00:08:02,780 --> 00:08:07,320
and all of a sudden, a second-order force becomes a first-order force.

104
00:08:07,840 --> 00:08:14,240
After the spectacular collapse of the Tacoma Narrows Bridge, other bridges with similar construction were stiffened,

105
00:08:15,020 --> 00:08:19,100
and they remain open to traffic today, almost 60 years later.

106
00:08:19,960 --> 00:08:23,600
The Tacoma Narrows Bridge disaster forced engineers from then on

107
00:08:23,601 --> 00:08:27,260
to take aerodynamics into consideration when designing bridges.

108
00:08:34,580 --> 00:08:38,880
From bridge construction to public buildings like the Beauvais Cathedral,

109
00:08:39,480 --> 00:08:44,040
the buildings people use and rely on every day are still liable to fail.

110
00:08:44,041 --> 00:08:50,520
on the night of July 17th 1981

111
00:08:50,521 --> 00:08:53,900
1500 people were enjoying a tea dance

112
00:08:53,901 --> 00:08:56,260
in the lobby of the Kansas City Hyatt Hotel

113
00:08:56,261 --> 00:08:59,820
because it was the biggest crowd to date for the new building

114
00:08:59,821 --> 00:09:02,600
stresses from weight were higher than they had ever been

115
00:09:02,601 --> 00:09:09,640
this animation helps explain what happened

116
00:09:09,641 --> 00:09:13,860
the hotel lobby featured three walkways

117
00:09:13,861 --> 00:09:16,240
suspended in levels over the main floor.

118
00:09:17,460 --> 00:09:18,980
This is the way it was originally designed.

119
00:09:19,620 --> 00:09:22,760
See, the load never should have gone through this box beam.

120
00:09:23,900 --> 00:09:26,920
See, the original design was the load go all the way through to the ceiling.

121
00:09:27,980 --> 00:09:29,180
But you see what they did here.

122
00:09:30,120 --> 00:09:32,440
The quality of the weld here and the quality of the weld here

123
00:09:32,441 --> 00:09:34,180
determines whether or not this connection stays up.

124
00:09:38,180 --> 00:09:40,040
But good welds are not.

125
00:09:40,300 --> 00:09:43,120
They were never designed to carry such loads.

126
00:09:43,860 --> 00:09:47,620
When the rubble was cleared away, 114 people lay dead.

127
00:09:48,120 --> 00:09:50,120
How did construction like this slip by?

128
00:09:50,740 --> 00:09:52,240
Weren't there building inspectors?

129
00:09:52,680 --> 00:09:56,740
Roger McCarthy investigated on behalf of the architect's insurance company.

130
00:09:57,460 --> 00:10:02,220
Yes, this was a 600-room hotel, and the building inspection took 27 minutes.

131
00:10:02,980 --> 00:10:08,020
In the years following the collapse, a blizzard of lawsuits flew in every direction,

132
00:10:08,540 --> 00:10:11,340
and two structural engineers lost their licenses.

133
00:10:12,060 --> 00:10:15,740
Incredible as the Hyatt collapse and the Tacoma Narrows Bridge may seem,

134
00:10:15,900 --> 00:10:19,740
they are just the most recent engineering disasters in public building.

135
00:10:20,260 --> 00:10:22,900
If history teaches anything, they won't be the last.

136
00:10:24,380 --> 00:10:30,640
With 114 people killed, the Hyatt disaster was the worst single structure collapse

137
00:10:30,641 --> 00:10:34,100
in terms of loss of life in United States history.

138
00:10:35,040 --> 00:10:39,000
Engineering disasters will continue in a moment on Modern Marvels.

139
00:10:41,340 --> 00:10:48,640
Water, the birthplace of life, sustainer of life, ancient highway since the first ore was carved.

140
00:10:49,560 --> 00:10:55,340
Since the dawn of time, man has tried to tame the power of water, to turn it to his own purpose.

141
00:10:55,900 --> 00:11:00,420
Sometimes he succeeds. When he fails, it can be spectacular.

142
00:11:01,840 --> 00:11:06,860
As history progressed, builders of ever more complex systems and machines were discovering

143
00:11:06,861 --> 00:11:11,260
that the more complex the system, the more things there are to go wrong.

144
00:11:12,360 --> 00:11:17,140
The Titanic, I think, is one of the most classic illustrations of that.

145
00:11:17,700 --> 00:11:22,080
Had just any number of things, just one thing been different,

146
00:11:22,680 --> 00:11:23,900
the disaster wouldn't have happened.

147
00:11:25,120 --> 00:11:29,020
She was the biggest, the most luxurious, the most expensive.

148
00:11:29,840 --> 00:11:33,440
Millionaires used their influence to get tickets on her maiden voyage

149
00:11:33,441 --> 00:11:35,300
from Southampton to New York.

150
00:11:36,240 --> 00:11:41,600
Then at 11.40 p.m., the fifth day out, she struck an iceberg.

151
00:11:42,820 --> 00:11:46,660
1,507 souls went down to the bottom.

152
00:11:47,540 --> 00:11:52,420
Yet the total area of the holes in her side were only about the size of a refrigerator.

153
00:11:53,540 --> 00:11:57,000
What happened to sink the unsinkable ship?

154
00:11:57,600 --> 00:12:00,780
The killer is when the top of the bulkhead goes below the waterline,

155
00:12:00,980 --> 00:12:04,900
that allows the next bulkhead in the cascade to be flooded.

156
00:12:04,901 --> 00:12:10,660
See, so what happens, as soon as the weight of the front pulls the top of the next bulkhead below the waterline,

157
00:12:10,880 --> 00:12:13,560
then the water can cascade into the following bulkheads.

158
00:12:14,220 --> 00:12:16,680
That's the mechanical cause of the disaster.

159
00:12:17,280 --> 00:12:19,300
But mystery still remains.

160
00:12:20,180 --> 00:12:23,220
How did the iceberg rip through the double steel hull?

161
00:12:24,380 --> 00:12:25,780
Not very tough steel.

162
00:12:25,960 --> 00:12:30,660
There's no question that the plate in the Titanic could not be used in a ship today.

163
00:12:31,420 --> 00:12:35,200
But we do see linear indications that look like the seams fail.

164
00:12:35,580 --> 00:12:36,900
Bad rivets or bad steel?

165
00:12:37,560 --> 00:12:39,200
We've pulled up a couple rivets.

166
00:12:39,620 --> 00:12:40,780
They don't look very good.

167
00:12:41,380 --> 00:12:44,640
So the rivets weren't very good, and the hull plate was not very good.

168
00:12:45,400 --> 00:12:51,480
The steel in the Titanic's hull was far inferior to modern steel in one crucial respect.

169
00:12:52,060 --> 00:12:56,560
Because of its high sulfur content, it became brittle as it got colder.

170
00:12:57,160 --> 00:12:58,820
That meant in the icy North Atlantic,

171
00:12:58,960 --> 00:13:02,160
the Titanic's hull was more prone to breaking rather than bending.

172
00:13:02,161 --> 00:13:06,800
And new studies indicate the three million rivets that held the ship together

173
00:13:06,801 --> 00:13:09,760
were made badly even by the standards of the time.

174
00:13:10,440 --> 00:13:13,180
Whole seams zipped open when she hit the iceberg.

175
00:13:13,880 --> 00:13:17,220
It's hard to imagine the most luxurious ship ever built.

176
00:13:17,860 --> 00:13:20,280
But underneath the gold leaf and mahogany,

177
00:13:20,440 --> 00:13:23,800
they used bad steel and poor rivets to hold her together.

178
00:13:26,560 --> 00:13:37,220
As in the triumphant Hoover Dam in Nevada, the power of water tamed can be breathtaking, unless it goes horribly wrong.

179
00:13:38,040 --> 00:13:42,240
Surrounded by some of the most beautiful scenery in the world, in 1972,

180
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:47,060
the Bureau of Reclamation began construction on the Teton Dam in Idaho,

181
00:13:47,061 --> 00:13:51,380
a project so ambitious they hoped it would change the scenery forever.

182
00:13:51,381 --> 00:13:56,860
it was a risky dam

183
00:13:56,861 --> 00:13:59,480
it was built on risky

184
00:13:59,481 --> 00:14:01,400
subsoil

185
00:14:01,401 --> 00:14:03,060
in an earthquake area

186
00:14:03,061 --> 00:14:04,100
but they pushed it

187
00:14:04,101 --> 00:14:07,100
work began in 1972

188
00:14:07,101 --> 00:14:08,820
after surveys were completed

189
00:14:08,821 --> 00:14:09,880
and plans drawn

190
00:14:09,881 --> 00:14:12,420
but critics charged the Bureau of Reclamation

191
00:14:12,421 --> 00:14:14,480
which built the dam moved too quickly

192
00:14:14,481 --> 00:14:16,420
they say they should have done

193
00:14:16,421 --> 00:14:17,800
far more preparation

194
00:14:17,801 --> 00:14:20,260
that they didn't show enough respect

195
00:14:20,261 --> 00:14:21,960
for the power of water.

196
00:14:23,700 --> 00:14:26,720
They went ahead like they went ahead with the Challenger launch.

197
00:14:27,260 --> 00:14:29,400
We're going to get this done. We're going to do it.

198
00:14:29,420 --> 00:14:30,460
It's going to be a great thing.

199
00:14:31,280 --> 00:14:32,120
They were wrong.

200
00:14:32,520 --> 00:14:34,800
As the earthen dam filled to capacity,

201
00:14:35,120 --> 00:14:39,240
deep inside, water was eroding the subsoil foundation of the dam.

202
00:14:40,100 --> 00:14:41,360
And there was another problem.

203
00:14:41,980 --> 00:14:44,480
Piping or water seepage within the dam itself

204
00:14:44,481 --> 00:14:46,500
was fatally weakening the structure,

205
00:14:47,300 --> 00:14:49,040
making it soggy and soft.

206
00:14:49,700 --> 00:14:54,520
Tiny wet spots began to appear on the face of the dam as it neared capacity.

207
00:14:55,260 --> 00:15:05,480
They got signs that they had not sufficiently sealed the base of the dam and that it was leaking.

208
00:15:06,860 --> 00:15:08,500
Another warning sign.

209
00:15:08,900 --> 00:15:13,760
High-pressure springs appeared in the earth thousands of feet downstream from the dam.

210
00:15:14,520 --> 00:15:18,040
On the dam face itself, the wet spots became leaks.

211
00:15:18,041 --> 00:15:23,020
On the morning of June 5, 1976, more leaks developed.

212
00:15:23,600 --> 00:15:28,980
A government photographer took this series of pictures as the wet spots turned into small holes,

213
00:15:29,320 --> 00:15:31,520
then larger holes in the face of the dam.

214
00:15:32,100 --> 00:15:34,520
The dam was collapsing before his eyes.

215
00:15:37,560 --> 00:15:44,440
Then suddenly, the 500-yard thick earthen wall collapsed like a sandcastle under a wave.

216
00:15:44,441 --> 00:15:49,040
Torrent washed away everything in 300 square miles.

217
00:15:49,780 --> 00:15:55,640
80 billion gallons of water totally destroyed two towns and killed 11 people.

218
00:16:01,860 --> 00:16:10,020
The failure of the Teton Dam led to a national review of federal rules regarding dam construction and inspection.

219
00:16:14,440 --> 00:16:21,220
Coming up next, the kind of accidents that strike fear into the hearts of all who ride in airplanes.

220
00:16:22,040 --> 00:16:23,500
Aviation disasters.

221
00:16:25,840 --> 00:16:30,140
The Titanic skipper was the most experienced captain in the company.

222
00:16:30,680 --> 00:16:35,080
He argued strongly for more lifeboats to be removed before they sailed,

223
00:16:35,760 --> 00:16:38,760
saying it was inconceivable they would ever be used.

224
00:16:39,460 --> 00:16:43,240
Engineering disasters will return on Modern Models.

225
00:16:44,440 --> 00:16:50,880
Mankind has a spirit that wants to rise and soar in space.

226
00:16:51,640 --> 00:16:55,840
Visionary engineers from Leonardo da Vinci all the way to the Wright brothers

227
00:16:55,841 --> 00:16:57,680
have made that dream come true.

228
00:16:58,960 --> 00:17:02,720
600 million passengers now take flight every year,

229
00:17:03,360 --> 00:17:05,800
soaring faster and higher than any bird.

230
00:17:05,801 --> 00:17:16,080
The first aviation disaster involved Daedalus and his son Icarus.

231
00:17:16,081 --> 00:17:20,180
In the Greek myth, Icarus pushed the envelope too far.

232
00:17:20,980 --> 00:17:26,980
When he began to approach the sun, the wax on his wings failed, and he tumbled back to earth.

233
00:17:26,981 --> 00:17:38,600
The first important air disaster involved the hydrogen-filled Zeppelin, Hindenburg.

234
00:17:38,920 --> 00:17:44,220
After several round trips from Germany to the airship landing field at Lakehurst, New Jersey,

235
00:17:44,860 --> 00:17:51,200
on May 6, 1937, the Hindenburg arrived just after a fierce electrical storm.

236
00:17:52,220 --> 00:17:56,840
Camera crews were there to chronicle what happened when she dropped her mooring lines to Earth.

237
00:17:56,980 --> 00:17:59,960
This is terrible. This is one of the worst catastrophes in the world.

238
00:18:00,360 --> 00:18:05,900
Oh, it's a place 20, oh, 400 or 500 feet into the sky.

239
00:18:06,080 --> 00:18:08,500
It's a terrific crash, ladies and gentlemen.

240
00:18:08,501 --> 00:18:10,180
It's a smoke and it's slaves now.

241
00:18:10,420 --> 00:18:14,520
And the flamers crashing to the ground, not quite to the boring mass.

242
00:18:14,740 --> 00:18:16,960
It's all the humanity and all the...

243
00:18:16,961 --> 00:18:21,640
Many suspected political sabotage as the cause of the disaster.

244
00:18:22,120 --> 00:18:24,740
But more likely was a combination of failures.

245
00:18:24,741 --> 00:18:30,440
some form of electrical discharge resulting from the ship's ropes touching the ground after a thunderstorm,

246
00:18:31,180 --> 00:18:34,820
coupled with a leak in one of the hydrogen bags that allowed the ship to float.

247
00:18:35,560 --> 00:18:40,260
The discharge most likely caused a spark that ignited the extremely explosive hydrogen.

248
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:43,700
The entire ship was incinerated in seconds.

249
00:18:45,780 --> 00:18:49,700
The horrifying footage made such a deep impression around the world

250
00:18:49,701 --> 00:18:52,980
that after the Hindenburg, Zeppelins were completely abandoned.

251
00:18:52,981 --> 00:18:57,140
yet only 36 people had died in the fiery crash.

252
00:19:01,240 --> 00:19:04,960
A modern airliner is a device with a million parts

253
00:19:04,961 --> 00:19:08,460
that pushes fragile humans to the edge of space

254
00:19:08,461 --> 00:19:09,900
and the edge of the sound barrier.

255
00:19:10,560 --> 00:19:12,880
The failure of one or two of those parts

256
00:19:12,881 --> 00:19:14,380
at precisely the right moment

257
00:19:14,381 --> 00:19:17,420
and the whole magnificent system falls to earth

258
00:19:17,421 --> 00:19:19,120
often killing hundreds.

259
00:19:20,020 --> 00:19:25,920
One of the co-pilots came rushing back several times, back and forth.

260
00:19:26,920 --> 00:19:31,920
And you could look at the eyes of the stewardess and know that we were in serious trouble.

261
00:19:32,680 --> 00:19:34,120
Just look at their eyes.

262
00:19:35,140 --> 00:19:37,720
Mary and Jim Call did what few people have done.

263
00:19:38,120 --> 00:19:40,460
They survived an airline disaster.

264
00:19:41,540 --> 00:19:47,980
Theirs came aboard one of the troubled early McDonnell Douglas DC-10s on a 1989 flight to Chicago.

265
00:19:48,880 --> 00:19:53,560
Everything seemed perfectly routine until a loud bang rocked the plane.

266
00:19:54,220 --> 00:19:55,960
Soon the pilot made an announcement.

267
00:19:56,560 --> 00:20:02,200
After we did the big dive and we heard the big boom,

268
00:20:02,900 --> 00:20:04,940
he told us that we lost the tail engine.

269
00:20:06,540 --> 00:20:10,860
We really weren't aware that there was a big gaping hole in the back of the plane.

270
00:20:11,620 --> 00:20:12,780
He said he wasn't going to kid us.

271
00:20:12,900 --> 00:20:14,800
It was going to be rough, very rough.

272
00:20:15,000 --> 00:20:16,280
He said he wasn't going to kid us.

273
00:20:16,281 --> 00:20:23,180
What Jim and Mary didn't know was that the number two engine in the tail had lost a fan disc.

274
00:20:23,620 --> 00:20:30,660
In an instant, the disintegrating fan's shrapnel punctured all three of the plane's redundant hydraulic lines,

275
00:20:31,040 --> 00:20:36,740
causing all the hydraulic fluid to leak out and at the same time chopped off a piece of the tail.

276
00:20:37,280 --> 00:20:41,160
With no hydraulics, the captain had no control of the aircraft.

277
00:20:41,960 --> 00:20:46,540
Other aircraft use a fourth separate line in case of accidents like this.

278
00:20:47,240 --> 00:20:50,140
At least one passenger knew exactly what was happening.

279
00:20:50,720 --> 00:20:53,060
He built commercial airliners for a living.

280
00:20:53,820 --> 00:20:56,720
I recognized some of the noises the aircraft was making.

281
00:20:57,260 --> 00:20:59,660
The hydraulic systems were sucking air.

282
00:21:00,280 --> 00:21:01,220
I just thought we're dead.

283
00:21:02,360 --> 00:21:04,060
No hydraulics, no control.

284
00:21:04,680 --> 00:21:07,020
No wing surfaces, no brakes, no nothing.

285
00:21:07,021 --> 00:21:15,400
For over 40 unforgettable minutes, this DC-10 carrying Bill Mackin and the calls hurtled through the sky.

286
00:21:15,960 --> 00:21:18,820
It almost turned over at least three times.

287
00:21:19,760 --> 00:21:25,160
Passengers watched in horror as the crew dumped excess fuel in preparation for a crash.

288
00:21:26,020 --> 00:21:28,520
Surprisingly, there was a little panic on board.

289
00:21:29,420 --> 00:21:32,000
Some crying, some people cried, some people prayed.

290
00:21:32,001 --> 00:21:36,320
There was hysterics. There was some silent sobbing.

291
00:21:37,020 --> 00:21:43,400
People saying the Lord's Prayer, but not the mass hysteria that you would expect.

292
00:21:43,401 --> 00:21:44,860
No, there was not hysteria at all.

293
00:21:45,060 --> 00:21:46,620
People were very, very calm.

294
00:21:46,980 --> 00:21:48,080
Looking at each other.

295
00:21:50,100 --> 00:21:52,360
A TV crew was standing by.

296
00:21:53,240 --> 00:21:55,600
Through an incredible piece of flying by the captain,

297
00:21:56,100 --> 00:21:59,900
the huge wide body controlled only by the two outboard engines

298
00:21:59,901 --> 00:22:03,000
miraculously made it to the Sioux City Airport.

299
00:22:03,640 --> 00:22:05,980
It came to earth only yards short of the runway.

300
00:22:07,020 --> 00:22:11,480
We were just like being tossed like rag dolls every which way I

301
00:22:12,060 --> 00:22:16,180
Got knocked up and I saw the flame shoot over top of my left

302
00:22:17,260 --> 00:22:18,840
left side of

303
00:22:18,841 --> 00:22:25,520
The cabin all the overhead bins opened up everything come flying out through the cabin and we saw the fire and

304
00:22:26,400 --> 00:22:31,740
Fireball come down first class section and I could feel the heat and see the fire

305
00:22:31,980 --> 00:22:34,080
But I never did get burned every got time

306
00:22:35,080 --> 00:22:41,060
Incredibly, 185 people, including Bill Macken, the calls, and the captain,

307
00:22:41,220 --> 00:22:43,380
walked away from this fiery disaster.

308
00:22:44,140 --> 00:22:46,920
Sadly, 111 weren't so lucky.

309
00:22:51,340 --> 00:22:56,300
Just as important as careful design is proper maintenance of aircraft.

310
00:22:57,220 --> 00:23:03,460
Take the Aloha Airline 737 descending for a landing at Honolulu in April of 1988.

311
00:23:04,080 --> 00:23:08,640
Suddenly, 18 feet of the top of the plane simply tore off.

312
00:23:09,580 --> 00:23:13,280
A flight attendant who was not in her seat was sucked out into the sky.

313
00:23:14,240 --> 00:23:17,900
But after an emergency landing, no other lives were lost.

314
00:23:19,280 --> 00:23:21,760
Design flaw and a maintenance disaster.

315
00:23:22,260 --> 00:23:23,760
Obviously, a design flaw.

316
00:23:23,880 --> 00:23:30,440
And what is really sad is other operators and the manufacturer had figured that out.

317
00:23:30,441 --> 00:23:36,040
there were actually kits available to go back and retrofit and check for those things.

318
00:23:36,560 --> 00:23:41,560
The problem turned out to be simple metal fatigue associated with the use of aluminum,

319
00:23:41,820 --> 00:23:45,900
which is unlike steel in one crucial respect, fatigue life.

320
00:23:46,620 --> 00:23:48,940
Aluminum does not have an infinite fatigue life.

321
00:23:49,200 --> 00:23:53,680
If I took a bar that it took 10,000 pounds to break and I started pulling on it at 2,000 pounds,

322
00:23:53,860 --> 00:23:57,360
after a while, it would go, snap, and I would have two pieces.

323
00:23:57,940 --> 00:24:04,280
Every time an aluminum passenger jet reaches altitude, it is pressurized inside like a balloon.

324
00:24:04,800 --> 00:24:06,900
When it descends, the pressure is released.

325
00:24:07,380 --> 00:24:13,000
If this process continues indefinitely without maintenance, sooner or later the balloon bursts.

326
00:24:13,820 --> 00:24:20,020
In airplanes, it becomes a concern around 30,000 to 50,000 takeoff and landing cycles.

327
00:24:20,580 --> 00:24:23,600
The first thing that happens is that cracks form in the skin.

328
00:24:23,601 --> 00:24:33,620
There were a couple people who actually saw the crack, or they boarded, and they just assumed, and I also assumed, that we could just trust that somebody had seen that

329
00:24:33,621 --> 00:24:35,840
and the government was doing its job.

330
00:24:39,580 --> 00:24:51,940
Then there's the case of TWA 800, which went down in the sea on its way from New York to Paris on July 17, 1996, taking all 230 lives.

331
00:24:51,941 --> 00:24:59,460
In the weeks and months after the crash, there was widespread speculation that the plane might have been brought down by a missile.

332
00:25:00,260 --> 00:25:08,400
The speculation reached such a fever pitch that the FBI commissioned the CIA to create this film explaining their version of events.

333
00:25:09,280 --> 00:25:12,380
They hoped to put to rest the missile theory once and for all.

334
00:25:13,200 --> 00:25:19,000
The FBI believes faulty wiring caused a spark in an empty fuel tank igniting an explosion.

335
00:25:19,100 --> 00:25:21,920
The film was shown on television nationwide.

336
00:25:22,700 --> 00:25:34,321
Just after the aircraft exploded, it pitched up abruptly and climbed several thousand feet from its last recorded altitude of about 13,800 feet to a maximum altitude

337
00:25:34,322 --> 00:25:35,800
of about 17,000 feet.

338
00:25:36,760 --> 00:25:47,580
The FBI, as well as many in the industry, believe that two kinds of wire known to be trouble for some time may have failed and caused a spontaneous explosion on board.

339
00:25:48,200 --> 00:25:51,860
The wire, though still in use, is quietly being phased out.

340
00:25:51,940 --> 00:25:56,040
The United States military pulled it off their planes.

341
00:25:56,041 --> 00:25:57,940
The Navy pulled it off their planes.

342
00:25:57,941 --> 00:26:02,440
In fact, planes they couldn't get it off of, they grounded in the early 80s, mothballed them.

343
00:26:02,441 --> 00:26:05,440
They made sure it was not on Air Force One.

344
00:26:05,441 --> 00:26:12,440
But for the rest of us, the Federal Aviation's position is, it is not a threat to safety.

345
00:26:12,441 --> 00:26:21,440
TWA Flight 800 went down in about 120 feet of water 10 miles off the coast of Long Island, New York.

346
00:26:21,441 --> 00:26:22,141
U.S.

347
00:26:22,142 --> 00:26:27,360
Navy divers spent four months and 4,000 dives to do what many thought was impossible.

348
00:26:28,140 --> 00:26:31,140
They raised 90% of the shattered airliner,

349
00:26:31,700 --> 00:26:37,300
a plane that weighed 350,000 pounds empty from the bottom of the ocean.

350
00:26:37,840 --> 00:26:40,820
The plane was reconstructed in a warehouse on shore.

351
00:26:42,220 --> 00:26:45,820
The old McDonnell Douglas DC-10 is no longer being manufactured,

352
00:26:46,820 --> 00:26:50,760
and Boeing is phasing out its famed 747 in favor of the new 777,

353
00:26:50,761 --> 00:26:55,460
which carries the same passengers the same distance for a third less cost.

354
00:26:55,820 --> 00:26:59,200
Engineering disasters will return on Modern Marvels.

355
00:27:01,000 --> 00:27:03,880
In the checkered history of the human race,

356
00:27:04,320 --> 00:27:07,200
the machine makers, the divisors of things,

357
00:27:07,720 --> 00:27:11,500
have brought mankind some of its most soaring moments of glory.

358
00:27:12,720 --> 00:27:16,140
Among those moments certainly was the sight of a human being

359
00:27:16,141 --> 00:27:20,440
leaving his, our, footprint on the surface of the moon.

360
00:27:20,760 --> 00:27:26,560
I believe that this nation should commit itself to achieving the goal,

361
00:27:27,300 --> 00:27:33,120
before this decade is out, of landing a man on the moon and returning him safely to the Earth.

362
00:27:39,900 --> 00:27:45,240
The Apollo program was NASA's response to President Kennedy's challenge.

363
00:27:45,241 --> 00:27:52,740
The program was designed to progress in stages.

364
00:27:52,741 --> 00:27:57,200
Apollo 1 was staffed with some of America's most experienced astronauts,

365
00:27:57,760 --> 00:28:00,200
Gus Grissom, Ed White, and Roger Chaffee.

366
00:28:01,140 --> 00:28:07,480
On January 27, 1967, all three perished inside their training module on Earth

367
00:28:07,481 --> 00:28:11,680
when faulty wiring caused a spark in the oxygen-rich environment.

368
00:28:11,681 --> 00:28:18,460
though technicians outside the capsule knew about the fire and worked frantically to free the trapped astronauts

369
00:28:18,461 --> 00:28:23,000
it took five minutes to get the complex escape hatch open

370
00:28:23,001 --> 00:28:26,040
by then it was too late for the three men inside

371
00:28:26,041 --> 00:28:30,040
the disaster set the space program back over a year

372
00:28:30,041 --> 00:28:34,260
16 months later in 1969

373
00:28:34,261 --> 00:28:37,620
Apollo 11 landed on the moon

374
00:28:37,621 --> 00:28:49,620
This was an engineering triumph, perhaps beyond anything in history.

375
00:28:49,621 --> 00:28:58,620
That's one small step for man, one giant leap for mankind.

376
00:28:58,621 --> 00:29:04,620
But on the heels of triumph, just one year later, disaster struck again.

377
00:29:04,621 --> 00:29:06,620
Apollo 13.

378
00:29:06,621 --> 00:29:08,500
Okay, we've had a problem here.

379
00:29:09,640 --> 00:29:11,840
Wonderful accident, so to speak.

380
00:29:12,460 --> 00:29:18,680
Turned out to be an absolutely stunning recovery from that.

381
00:29:19,020 --> 00:29:23,780
What was to be America's third landing on the moon ended in near disaster

382
00:29:23,781 --> 00:29:27,420
when astronauts heard and felt an explosion on board.

383
00:29:28,080 --> 00:29:35,020
A few small things that went wrong that normally wouldn't have made any difference.

384
00:29:35,021 --> 00:29:40,540
and they came together in just a particular kind of way in that ship.

385
00:29:41,300 --> 00:29:45,040
It turned out that two of the three fuel cells were destroyed

386
00:29:45,041 --> 00:29:48,320
and both oxygen tanks were losing pressure.

387
00:29:49,180 --> 00:29:53,360
Ground control and the crew figured out a way to use the lunar lander as a life raft

388
00:29:53,361 --> 00:29:57,940
and four days later they rounded the moon and returned safely to Earth.

389
00:29:58,420 --> 00:30:04,760
Two or three failures that interact in a way that nobody anticipated they would interact.

390
00:30:05,020 --> 00:30:10,000
then they defeat the safety systems and bring the system down.

391
00:30:11,040 --> 00:30:16,460
Several small, unconnected engineering failures combined to cause this disaster.

392
00:30:17,220 --> 00:30:24,340
A heater thermostat supposed to be upgraded to accept 65 volts was overlooked and burned out above 28 volts.

393
00:30:24,840 --> 00:30:29,440
Several years before the flight, an oxygen tank had been dropped about two inches,

394
00:30:30,040 --> 00:30:32,320
further damaging the attached thermostat.

395
00:30:33,040 --> 00:30:36,840
When astronauts followed orders to stir the tanks, a routine procedure.

396
00:30:36,841 --> 00:30:44,220
The already damaged wiring melted, shorting out and causing a spark in an environment of pure oxygen.

397
00:30:44,920 --> 00:30:48,500
The resulting explosion blew the side of the spacecraft off.

398
00:30:49,040 --> 00:30:52,120
After Apollo 13, the astronauts said,

399
00:30:52,580 --> 00:30:56,820
we are trained all the time for the unexpected.

400
00:30:56,821 --> 00:31:04,500
They slammed things to us in the simulator that just never would think about in order to prepare us for this.

401
00:31:04,980 --> 00:31:13,500
But if they had thrown those interactions that happened that brought about Apollo 13 to us in the simulator,

402
00:31:14,060 --> 00:31:15,780
we would have walked off the program.

403
00:31:15,920 --> 00:31:17,460
We would have said, that's so far out.

404
00:31:17,740 --> 00:31:18,980
That could never happen.

405
00:31:18,981 --> 00:31:32,981
That same aerospace industry that could so inspire the people of Earth shattered our confidence and broke our hearts in an instant on January 28th, 1986.

406
00:31:36,640 --> 00:31:45,040
Few who watched the Challenger disaster live will ever forget that moment as the magnificent ship exploded and dropped into the sea.

407
00:31:45,880 --> 00:31:48,960
In the weeks that followed, sorrow turned to rage,

408
00:31:49,640 --> 00:31:52,420
as the true causes of the disaster became known.

409
00:31:53,380 --> 00:31:57,840
The immediate cause was a white-hot flame that burned through the side of a rocket motor.

410
00:31:57,841 --> 00:32:01,600
A puff of smoke is visible here as the burn-through begins.

411
00:32:02,440 --> 00:32:05,900
Only seconds later in the flight, the hot flare is clearly visible,

412
00:32:06,220 --> 00:32:08,140
burning through the side of the rocket motor.

413
00:32:08,780 --> 00:32:12,100
Like a blowtorch, the flare burned through the main fuel tank,

414
00:32:12,101 --> 00:32:17,560
igniting the liquid oxygen and hydrogen inside in a spectacular explosion.

415
00:32:18,700 --> 00:32:21,640
The flare had started at a joint in the rocket motor,

416
00:32:22,200 --> 00:32:24,280
joint sealed with rubber O-rings.

417
00:32:25,560 --> 00:32:28,080
NASA and its contractors had evidence

418
00:32:28,081 --> 00:32:32,900
that the rocket motor O-rings could fail below 53 degrees Fahrenheit.

419
00:32:33,700 --> 00:32:38,820
But NASA had already launched shuttles below 53 degrees and gotten away with it.

420
00:32:38,821 --> 00:32:41,440
Just because you got lucky doesn't mean the risk is reduced.

421
00:32:41,441 --> 00:32:46,360
And sure enough, the risks were still there, and the statistics caught up with them.

422
00:32:46,820 --> 00:32:54,360
On January 28, 1986, NASA decided to go ahead with the shuttle launch at only 36 degrees.

423
00:32:55,640 --> 00:32:59,200
There was solid ice all over the launch pad.

424
00:33:00,500 --> 00:33:04,760
Engineers involved with the O-rings did everything they could to stop the launch.

425
00:33:05,580 --> 00:33:08,320
On the fourth or fifth time, they pushed the envelope.

426
00:33:08,321 --> 00:33:13,840
So sure enough, almost exactly at the temperatures they said, those O-rings blew apart.

427
00:33:14,000 --> 00:33:19,080
And the tragedy of that is, besides the tragic souls lost on the Challenger themselves,

428
00:33:19,220 --> 00:33:23,440
there were people on the ground who warned about it, who were so upset about it,

429
00:33:23,460 --> 00:33:28,580
and who to this day are destroyed because they knew about it, tried to warn, and they were dismissed.

430
00:33:33,220 --> 00:33:36,660
In recent years, there was another American space disaster.

431
00:33:36,661 --> 00:33:41,440
this one did not cause any loss of life, though it was a huge financial mess.

432
00:33:42,100 --> 00:33:43,840
And it was unusual in another way.

433
00:33:44,260 --> 00:33:45,640
It had a happy ending.

434
00:33:46,820 --> 00:33:51,580
The Hubble Space Telescope was a bitter disappointment to scientists from the day it was launched.

435
00:33:52,180 --> 00:33:57,000
But the Hubble was already in orbit before scientists discovered the main mirror was distorted,

436
00:33:57,820 --> 00:34:01,940
producing only about one-third the resolution it was designed for.

437
00:34:03,380 --> 00:34:05,200
Several things had gone wrong.

438
00:34:05,201 --> 00:34:10,260
One, the mirror had been ground to almost unthinkably close tolerances,

439
00:34:10,700 --> 00:34:11,840
but in the wrong shape.

440
00:34:12,480 --> 00:34:16,100
Because its own weight distorted its shape in the gravity on Earth.

441
00:34:16,640 --> 00:34:19,920
Once freed of gravity, the mirror sprung into a slightly different shape.

442
00:34:20,260 --> 00:34:22,180
Critics say it should have been tested for that.

443
00:34:22,940 --> 00:34:23,940
We have an axiom here.

444
00:34:24,440 --> 00:34:26,440
One test is worth a thousand expert opinions.

445
00:34:27,320 --> 00:34:31,440
And this is a classic example where no one could figure out

446
00:34:31,441 --> 00:34:34,400
a very elegant way to test it in zero-G.

447
00:34:34,401 --> 00:34:35,640
so they didn't.

448
00:34:36,600 --> 00:34:37,780
NASA had two choices.

449
00:34:38,360 --> 00:34:41,000
They could abandon the hugely expensive project

450
00:34:41,001 --> 00:34:43,520
or they could send a crew up to fix it.

451
00:34:44,340 --> 00:34:46,840
Astronaut Story Musgrave got the job.

452
00:34:47,180 --> 00:34:50,000
When he arrived in space, the Hubble was a mess.

453
00:34:51,280 --> 00:34:54,200
Putting in some new rate gyros which had failed.

454
00:34:54,340 --> 00:34:56,640
Putting in the new wide-field planetary camera,

455
00:34:56,740 --> 00:35:00,280
the deployable optics bench to correct for the spherical aberration,

456
00:35:00,740 --> 00:35:02,040
new magnetometers.

457
00:35:02,780 --> 00:35:08,060
The computer had lost half of its memory, replacing a coprocessor to get the memory back.

458
00:35:08,480 --> 00:35:15,840
All of those kinds of tasks, if you added up the total amount of work that we had to do, it came out to five days.

459
00:35:16,300 --> 00:35:20,400
At the end of it, the results were better than anyone had dared hope for.

460
00:35:20,700 --> 00:35:29,680
Since Story Musgrave's $50 million overhaul, the Hubble Space Telescope has looked farther into space than any instrument in history.

461
00:35:29,681 --> 00:35:34,880
A huge, costly engineering disaster that was fixed.

462
00:35:37,360 --> 00:35:43,960
The repaired Hubble Space Telescope can now see 10.5 billion light years into the past.

463
00:35:44,540 --> 00:35:49,500
A distance that approaches the edges of the Big Bang at the beginning of the universe.

464
00:35:50,300 --> 00:35:53,860
Engineering disasters will return on Modern Marvels.

465
00:35:53,861 --> 00:36:03,520
According to a failure analysis study, the average American consumes so much energy every day,

466
00:36:04,060 --> 00:36:07,260
it would take 200 slaves to reproduce his lifestyle.

467
00:36:08,220 --> 00:36:10,920
Modern industry makes such luxury possible.

468
00:36:11,540 --> 00:36:16,300
But the energy generators, chemical and manufacturing plants that create so much luxury

469
00:36:16,301 --> 00:36:19,080
can spread instant death when they fail.

470
00:36:19,081 --> 00:36:28,340
Since the first mushroom cloud lit the sky in the New Mexico desert in 1945,

471
00:36:29,000 --> 00:36:30,980
the world has never been the same.

472
00:36:32,420 --> 00:36:37,780
Thirty-four years later, Three Mile Island, America's worst nuclear disaster.

473
00:36:38,580 --> 00:36:43,780
Three Mile Island had four fairly trivial failures.

474
00:36:43,781 --> 00:36:50,520
failures. All of them had ways to recover from. None were that serious. But they all

475
00:36:50,521 --> 00:36:52,189
happened together.

476
00:36:52,190 --> 00:36:58,960
And that led the operators, following their instructions, doing just what

477
00:36:58,961 --> 00:37:02,992
they should have done, to zig when they should have zagged.

478
00:37:02,993 --> 00:37:04,700
That caused the accident.

479
00:37:05,460 --> 00:37:12,860
It began on March 28th, 1979, when a cooling pump failed at TMI reactor number two.

480
00:37:13,780 --> 00:37:16,980
The reactor automatically shut down as it was supposed to,

481
00:37:17,460 --> 00:37:22,240
but then a relief valve stuck open, venting radioactive steam out to the atmosphere,

482
00:37:22,900 --> 00:37:25,540
after which the operators made a crucial mistake.

483
00:37:26,280 --> 00:37:29,240
They stopped the flow of water to the radioactive core.

484
00:37:29,860 --> 00:37:35,280
The hot uranium core quickly boiled off the remaining water and then began to melt.

485
00:37:36,220 --> 00:37:38,300
Although operators followed their training,

486
00:37:38,480 --> 00:37:42,140
they weren't aware of the interaction of the group of failures that was occurring.

487
00:37:43,140 --> 00:37:45,660
But when the core dried up and began to melt down,

488
00:37:46,160 --> 00:37:49,660
operators realized their mistake and resumed the coolant flow.

489
00:37:50,260 --> 00:37:54,180
But the fuel was so hot by then, it shattered on contact with the cool water.

490
00:37:55,320 --> 00:37:58,840
By the end of the accident, about half of the core had melted down.

491
00:37:59,740 --> 00:38:02,020
Almost all the radiation was contained.

492
00:38:02,021 --> 00:38:06,320
There was no loss of life and no large-scale release of radiation.

493
00:38:06,640 --> 00:38:11,540
But the disaster effectively doomed the nuclear power industry in America.

494
00:38:12,140 --> 00:38:26,140
Seven years later, April 26, 1986.

495
00:38:27,540 --> 00:38:31,340
The Ukraine is seen in this Russian documentary.

496
00:38:32,080 --> 00:38:35,800
Reactor number four at Chernobyl explodes and burns,

497
00:38:36,580 --> 00:38:39,220
pouring radioactive material into the atmosphere.

498
00:38:39,221 --> 00:38:46,220
31 people die on the spot. Children were found to be most vulnerable.

499
00:38:46,221 --> 00:38:59,220
In the days after the explosion, a cloud of radioactivity that started out 200 times stronger than the Hiroshima and Nagasaki bombs spread across Russia and Europe.

500
00:38:59,221 --> 00:39:09,933
Back at the site, firefighters facing certain death with superhuman courage put out the blaze and poured thousands of yards of concrete on the damaged building, trying

501
00:39:09,934 --> 00:39:11,280
to entomb it forever.

502
00:39:14,080 --> 00:39:21,240
Filmmaker Vladimir Shevchenko, who shot this incredible footage, was among those who died of radiation poisoning.

503
00:39:21,241 --> 00:39:27,240
As bad as Chernobyl was, it could have been much worse.

504
00:39:27,241 --> 00:39:34,240
If the wind had been blowing the other way, Chernobyl might have devastated the city of Kiev 80 miles to the south.

505
00:39:34,241 --> 00:39:39,240
Kiev's population is three and a quarter million.

506
00:39:39,241 --> 00:39:45,240
The explosions at Chernobyl occurred while an experiment was being run in the reactor.

507
00:39:45,241 --> 00:39:50,240
Incredibly, the experiment was conducted with the emergency water cooling system shut off.

508
00:39:50,241 --> 00:39:57,240
When the experiment went bad through a series of mistakes and miscalculations, a runaway reaction began.

509
00:39:57,241 --> 00:40:06,240
Out of control, the reaction shattered the fuel and eventually blew the roof off the building, exposing the core to the atmosphere.

510
00:40:06,241 --> 00:40:13,240
Chernobyl's Soviet-built reactor didn't have an immense, massive containment building like Three Mile Islands.

511
00:40:13,241 --> 00:40:17,512
islands. Chernobyl was the design disaster.

512
00:40:17,513 --> 00:40:22,380
I mean, first of all, the Soviets had much weaker

513
00:40:22,381 --> 00:40:27,456
and less safe designs for their nuclear power plants than even the U.S.

514
00:40:27,457 --> 00:40:28,600
manufacturers of

515
00:40:28,601 --> 00:40:30,141
nuclear power plants.

516
00:40:30,142 --> 00:40:35,500
And coupled with a whole culture of secrecy, cover-up, bureaucratic flank

517
00:40:35,501 --> 00:40:39,784
covering, it was a recipe for disaster. And it occurred.

518
00:40:39,785 --> 00:40:42,920
Nuclear power plants in the United States

519
00:40:42,921 --> 00:40:47,560
in Europe are designed and maintained to much higher standards than the ones at Chernobyl.

520
00:40:48,280 --> 00:40:56,040
But even so, could such an accident happen here? The NRC back about 15 years ago

521
00:40:57,080 --> 00:41:01,295
thought that in the next 20 years there'd be one meltdown all the way.

522
00:41:01,296 --> 00:41:02,680
It hasn't happened yet.

523
00:41:02,681 --> 00:41:05,560
When it happens, it'll happen, you know, without any alert.

524
00:41:12,920 --> 00:41:20,300
The worst industrial disaster in history didn't involve a nuclear reactor or a plane or a bridge.

525
00:41:22,880 --> 00:41:24,120
Bhopal, India.

526
00:41:24,920 --> 00:41:31,940
Late one night in December 1984, a Union carbide pesticide plant based in Bhopal had an accident.

527
00:41:33,580 --> 00:41:36,220
It was right next to a large community.

528
00:41:36,221 --> 00:41:44,040
The community didn't have almost a roof over their head because it's just a shanty town.

529
00:41:44,320 --> 00:41:45,200
It was very open.

530
00:41:46,040 --> 00:41:48,120
Happened at night. Everybody was there.

531
00:41:48,580 --> 00:41:51,020
There was no warning for this accident.

532
00:41:51,580 --> 00:41:56,900
And when the accident happened, the people in the plant ran the other way and didn't warn anybody.

533
00:41:58,200 --> 00:42:04,240
Somehow, 50,000 pounds of deadly methyl isocyanate gas escaped from the plant,

534
00:42:04,241 --> 00:42:08,460
spreading across the sleeping city of one million souls.

535
00:42:09,480 --> 00:42:12,100
Between 2,000 and 6,000 people died instantly,

536
00:42:12,760 --> 00:42:13,760
most in their beds.

537
00:42:14,700 --> 00:42:17,560
Thousands more died later or were blinded.

538
00:42:18,620 --> 00:42:22,360
Well, Powell was just a very sloppy operation of the plant.

539
00:42:22,840 --> 00:42:26,600
First of all, it shouldn't have been positioned and sited in that area

540
00:42:26,601 --> 00:42:28,360
with huge population density.

541
00:42:28,361 --> 00:42:30,820
You know, you put that in a more remote area.

542
00:42:30,821 --> 00:42:36,800
But basically it was negligence up and down the line in terms of the operation of the plant.

543
00:42:37,920 --> 00:42:44,320
The underground tanks holding the poison were triple the size of any other tanks in the world for the same poison.

544
00:42:45,080 --> 00:42:47,460
The tanks were overfilled. They were too hot.

545
00:42:48,220 --> 00:42:52,500
When water hit the poison through leaking valves, a runaway reaction occurred.

546
00:42:53,360 --> 00:42:58,980
The devices and systems designed to neutralize it were either not functioning or had been removed.

547
00:42:58,981 --> 00:43:09,398
The Indian government initially demanded three billion dollars in damages and said they would prosecute Union Carbide executives for the world's worst industrial

548
00:43:09,399 --> 00:43:10,099
disaster.

549
00:43:10,100 --> 00:43:19,980
But by 1996 Union Carbide was paying a reduced settlement of 470 million and the executives had avoided criminal prosecution.

550
00:43:19,981 --> 00:43:26,980
At his core, the human animal is a builder.

551
00:43:26,981 --> 00:43:31,980
In all ages, among all races, that has always been true.

552
00:43:31,981 --> 00:43:36,980
Man's path out of the cave was littered with tools.

553
00:43:36,981 --> 00:43:39,980
But we have entered a new era in history.

554
00:43:39,981 --> 00:43:42,980
Our devices have grown in complexity to the point

555
00:43:42,981 --> 00:43:46,980
that we can no longer anticipate everything that could possibly go wrong.

556
00:43:46,981 --> 00:43:53,980
The more complex the system, the more likely an unforeseen interaction of small failures

557
00:43:53,981 --> 00:43:55,980
will cause a disaster.

558
00:43:55,981 --> 00:43:57,980
And this is to be feared.

559
00:43:57,981 --> 00:44:04,980
Because the genie that lives inside our most powerful machines, our nuclear reactors, our

560
00:44:04,981 --> 00:44:08,980
chemical plants, will kill us if it can escape.

561
00:44:08,981 --> 00:44:15,980
For the first time in history, engineers can wipe out whole cities by mistake.

